From 9805a1c7e973bdb23a688500c55a39cc63496230 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Solhex <solhex@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 15:33:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] ytm_bridge: run on Windows against Windows media sessions

The bridge was Linux-only: MPRIS through playerctl for metadata and commands,
swaymsg + vkkey for the like shortcut. Split the PC side into a backend per
platform and add a Windows one, so the same Flipper app works there with
Firefox and YouTube Music.

Windows backend (Windows media sessions / SMTC through WinRT):
- title, artist, playback state and cover art come from the session itself
- play/pause/next/previous: Firefox ignores the session's transport methods and
  returns success anyway, so the same WM_APPCOMMAND the media keys produce is
  posted at Firefox's hidden 'Firefox Media Keys' window
- seek types YouTube Music's l/h shortcut (+-10 s, since Firefox has no seek)
- volume moves that app's slider in the Windows mixer (pycaw)
- like reuses the shift+= approach, with SendInput and focus restore
- a session manager freezes the state it was requested with, so it is
  re-requested every 400 ms and right after a command
- Firefox reports no position and its timeline can carry the previous track's
  length, so the timeline is only trusted once the position is seen to move

Also: --pair, --dump (no Flipper needed) and --cmd for testing, a cached BLE
address instead of bluetoothctl, a radio-off retry, MTU handled through
mtu_size on platforms that negotiate it themselves, and line-buffered logs.
